<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>MUI.Web</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>MUI.Web</AssemblyName>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\MUI.Catalog\MUI.Catalog.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\MUI.Crawler\MUI.Crawler.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\MUI.Crawl\MUI.Crawl.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\MUI.Discovery\MUI.Discovery.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Markdig" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The hand-written reference section (spec §9). The prose lives at the repository root, outside
    this project, because it is content rather than code: a reviewer reads content/reference/*.md as
    prose, and somebody who does not write C# can correct a codebase description without opening the
    rendering shell.

    Embedded rather than copied beside the binary, so the pages ship inside the assembly. There is
    then no content root to resolve, no file that can be present in one deployment and missing in
    another, and no way for a running site to serve a page that is not in the repository — which is
    what "versioned in git" has to mean when it is the substitute for wiki governance.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\..\content\reference\*.md"
                      Link="Content\reference\%(Filename)%(Extension)" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Blazor *static* server rendering: components render to plain HTML on the server and ship no
    WebAssembly and no required script. That is what lets ?plain=1 be the same view models with a
    different renderer, and the facet panel be a real GET form that works with scripting off
    (spec §9). Interactivity, if it is ever needed, is added per-island rather than page-wide.

    The crawler runs in this same process as a BackgroundService (spec §4.11), which is why this
    project references MUI.Crawl and MUI.Discovery as well as the catalogue.
  -->

</Project>
